README-GREP -- six ways to search a file, and which to takeTake `grep'.  It needs no Microware module, it has six files ofdocumentation here, and its regular expressions are the ones you know.Add `bmg' if you search big files for fixed strings.THE SIX-------  file      bytes  cio  docs          what it is  -----------------------------------------------------------------  grep      72364  no   DOC/grep (6)  GNU grep 2.0 -- regular expressions  ggrep     53124  yes  grep's        another build of GNU grep 2.0  fgrep     19960  yes  DOC/fgrep (3) fixed strings, no expressions  bmg        6838  yes  its -? help   Boyer-Moore, fixed strings, fast  lgrep      2446  yes  DOC/lgrep     file names, from your OS-9's grep  gep        3654  yes  --            expressions read from a fileEach module is named after its file, so you can load them all together.All but `grep' need `cio', which ships on this disk (DOC/README-CIO).Take it with any of them you copy elsewhere.Near relatives, in DOC/INDEX: `agrep' forgives spelling, `sgrep'substitutes, `wns' prints a window of lines round each match.TAKE THIS IF------------  grep    you want one and no decisions.  `-c' counts, `-v' inverts.  bmg     you search something large for a fixed string.  It skips          rather than scans, and its exit status answers: `bmg apple file'          prints every matching line and exits 0; no match prints nothing          and exits 1.  `-f' reads the strings from a file.  fgrep   the same job as bmg, with documentation, at three times the          size.  lgrep   you want only the names of the files that match, to hand to          another command.  It runs your own OS-9's `grep -l' and strips          what that adds, so load that grep first (`load /h1/CMDS/grep').          With this disk's GNU grep it prints nothing.  ggrep   `grep' will not run for you.  gep     you have many expressions.  They come from a file named with          `-e', never from the command line:              gep -e=<patternfile> <file>          Given a pattern and a file the way grep takes them, it answers          `gep: more than one path specified'.IF YOU CAN TAKE ONLY ONE FILE`grep'.  If 72K is too much, `bmg' -- fixed strings only, and it needs`cio'.